26
00:05:08.218 --> 00:05:10.373
<i>Dr. Ryland Grace.</i>

27
00:05:10.626 --> 00:05:12.626
<i>End of manifest.</i>

28
00:05:15.172 --> 00:05:19.663
I can't be the only
person here. Right?

29
00:05:19.688 --> 00:05:20.988
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

30
00:05:21.013 --> 00:05:23.721
No! No. No-No. Not the pilot.

31
00:05:23.746 --> 00:05:27.513
Uh call, uh, Houston.

32
00:05:27.538 --> 00:05:28.750
<i>Unknown command.</i>

33
00:05:28.775 --> 00:05:31.202
Operation Go Home...

34
00:05:31.227 --> 00:05:33.506
- in effect.
- <i>Invalid operation.</i>

35
00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.709
That's the sun! There it is.

36
00:05:37.734 --> 00:05:40.546
So, what are we,
like, Neptune-ish?

37
00:05:41.779 --> 00:05:44.740
Let's crank up the
radio and call Earth.

38
00:05:44.765 --> 00:05:51.919
<i>Current transmission time to Earth is 11
years, 10 months, 14 days and 6 hours.</i>

39
00:05:51.944 --> 00:05:54.053
No. Nope.

40
00:05:54.078 --> 00:05:56.193
You're wrong.
I need a map.

41
00:05:58.261 --> 00:05:59.628
Oh.

42
00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:00.918
Uh

43
00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:04.165
That's so far.

44
00:06:16.112 --> 00:06:17.833
That's not our sun.

45
00:06:21.583 --> 00:06:23.349
That's not our sun.

46
00:06:37.081 --> 00:06:39.553
Where is the fuel?

47
00:06:44.895 --> 00:06:45.996
Wait.

48
00:07:20.304 --> 00:07:23.298
<i>We are just minutes
away from the fly-by</i>

49
00:07:23.323 --> 00:07:25.408
<i>through the Petrova
line near Venus.</i>

50
00:07:25.433 --> 00:07:28.303
<i>So, what can you tell us
about the ArcLight probe?</i>

51
00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:31.723
<i>What do we expect to find?</i>

52
00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.252
<i>The image we see will be coming
from the onboard microscope.</i>

53
00:07:39.408 --> 00:07:42.230
Oh! Oh my God.

54
00:07:46.242 --> 00:07:48.678
<i>Sorry. I have to go now.</i>

55
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:02.906
♪ Sound waves are physical. ♪

56
00:08:02.931 --> 00:08:07.386
♪ And at different frequencies, ♪

57
00:08:07.411 --> 00:08:10.491
♪ they make different pattern-- ♪

58
00:08:10.516 --> 00:08:11.510
♪ No, pattern-- ♪

59
00:08:11.535 --> 00:08:12.936
♪ They make different patt-- ♪

60
00:08:12.961 --> 00:08:15.683
Olivia, can you help
me out? There we go!

61
00:08:16.454 --> 00:08:17.555
Whoa!

62
00:08:18.976 --> 00:08:19.877
Yeah.

63
00:08:19.902 --> 00:08:21.937
Are they really eating the sun?

64
00:08:21.962 --> 00:08:23.971
The space dots?

65
00:08:23.996 --> 00:08:26.279
That is a great
question, uh, Rekha.

66
00:08:26.304 --> 00:08:29.525
And I'd be curious what
your parents think about that.

67
00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.296
I'm not sure it
should come from me.

68
00:08:31.321 --> 00:08:34.230
Hey! Who wants to play a game of

69
00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:35.957
the bean bag is lava!

70
00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:38.320
Lava! Lava! Lava!

71
00:08:38.345 --> 00:08:41.555
What is the speed
of light, Olivia?

72
00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:42.882
Uh

73
00:08:42.907 --> 00:08:44.234
It's melting your hands, Olivia!

74
00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:46.789
186,000 miles per second!

75
00:08:46.814 --> 00:08:48.686
Oh! Give her a
round of applause.

76
00:08:48.711 --> 00:08:50.813
That is awesome.

77
00:08:51.288 --> 00:08:53.572
What is the Petrova line?

78
00:08:54.353 --> 00:08:55.433
Pass!

79
00:08:55.458 --> 00:08:56.946
You can't pass!

80
00:08:56.971 --> 00:08:58.057
I just did.

81
00:08:58.082 --> 00:09:00.385
Lava! Lava! Lava!

82
00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:01.709
Come on, guys.

83
00:09:02.038 --> 00:09:04.354
Two years ago...

84
00:09:04.379 --> 00:09:09.327
a radio telescope enthusiast
named Irina Petrova,...

85
00:09:09.352 --> 00:09:12.500
she noticed that there
was a streak of infrared light

86
00:09:12.525 --> 00:09:14.411
from the sun to Venus.

87
00:09:14.436 --> 00:09:17.272
And that is now known
as the Petrova line.

88
00:09:17.297 --> 00:09:19.657
- That's it.
- That's the dots?

89
00:09:20.143 --> 00:09:22.836
- They think so.
- What dots?

90
00:09:26.016 --> 00:09:27.706
These dots.

91
00:09:28.038 --> 00:09:30.130
They sent a probe
up to the Petrova line,

92
00:09:30.155 --> 00:09:32.473
and this is what they found.

93
00:09:33.671 --> 00:09:36.175
Are they eating the sun?

94
00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.012
They seem to be...

95
00:09:39.037 --> 00:09:43.608
dimming the sun a tiny,
teeny-weeny, little bit.

96
00:09:43.947 --> 00:09:45.348
So, no big whoop.

97
00:09:45.373 --> 00:09:47.131
It's a small whoop.

98
00:09:47.156 --> 00:09:48.948
It's a small-to-medium whoop.

99
00:09:49.306 --> 00:09:53.025
Over the next 30
years, the Earth could...

100
00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.646
cool maybe 10 to...

101
00:09:56.671 --> 00:09:58.271
15 degrees.

102
00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:00.554
So it <i>is</i> a big whoop.

103
00:10:00.579 --> 00:10:03.091
I heard that all
the crops will die,

104
00:10:03.116 --> 00:10:04.548
the economy will free-fall,

105
00:10:04.573 --> 00:10:06.554
and half the Earth
will die of starvation.

106
00:10:07.545 --> 00:10:10.100
- Well.
- We're all gonna die!

107
00:10:10.125 --> 00:10:11.242
Yeah, it's true.

108
00:10:11.267 --> 00:10:12.541
You're forgetting something.

109
00:10:12.566 --> 00:10:16.100
Guys, guys! That's <i>if</i> that
were to happen. Right?

110
00:10:16.206 --> 00:10:17.951
They're gonna figure this out.

111
00:10:17.976 --> 00:10:24.227
Right now, the best minds
in the entire world are on it.

112
00:10:48.567 --> 00:10:50.625
<i>Good morning, Dr. Grace.</i>

113
00:10:52.309 --> 00:10:54.012
I'm up!

114
00:12:32.609 --> 00:12:33.931
Knock, knock.

115
00:12:35.732 --> 00:12:36.733
Who's there?

116
00:12:36.874 --> 00:12:38.175
Not good at jokes.

117
00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:39.969
Not good at jokes who?

118
00:12:43.528 --> 00:12:44.988
Dr. Grace?

119
00:12:46.917 --> 00:12:47.804
Maybe.

120
00:12:47.829 --> 00:12:49.722
Eva Stratt. I'm with
the Petrova Task Force.

121
00:12:49.747 --> 00:12:51.048
I need your help.

122
00:12:51.707 --> 00:12:54.103
- Me.
- Did you write this?

123
00:12:57.039 --> 00:12:57.677
Oh.

124
00:12:57.702 --> 00:12:59.411
I'm interested in
this section here.

125
00:12:59.436 --> 00:13:01.340
- Uh
- Page 31.

126
00:13:01.365 --> 00:13:05.843
"The Goldilocks Zone Is For Idiots:
Why Everyone Is Wrong About Life."

127
00:13:06.262 --> 00:13:07.631
That was a long time ago.

128
00:13:07.656 --> 00:13:08.869
Do you stand by what you wrote?

129
00:13:08.894 --> 00:13:11.750
I was fired for standing
by what I wrote.

130
00:13:11.869 --> 00:13:15.066
You were fired for calling the
leading scholar in your field

131
00:13:15.091 --> 00:13:18.824
"a staggering waste of carbon" at the
UNESCO conference in Denmark.

132
00:13:18.849 --> 00:13:21.021
You heard about that, huh?

133
00:13:21.204 --> 00:13:23.285
Look, I don't know what this is,

134
00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.324
but, uh, I think I'm not s--

135
00:13:26.349 --> 00:13:27.516
That's lava.

136
00:13:27.664 --> 00:13:29.161
- It's not.
- Okay.

137
00:13:29.713 --> 00:13:31.535
No one in your field
wants anything to do

138
00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.141
with you because you
refused to back down

139
00:13:33.166 --> 00:13:34.974
from a very unpopular
view, and I can

140
00:13:34.999 --> 00:13:36.568
give you a chance to
prove them all wrong.

141
00:13:36.593 --> 00:13:39.342
- Uh Kevin, no running.
- I'm not.

142
00:13:39.367 --> 00:13:43.810
Well, joke's on them
because I don't even care. So

143
00:13:44.589 --> 00:13:45.820
I think you do care.

144
00:13:45.845 --> 00:13:47.502
You're just running away
because you're scared.

145
00:13:47.624 --> 00:13:48.906
No, I'm not.

146
00:13:51.001 --> 00:13:54.047
Do you still believe water is
unnecessary for life to evolve?

147
00:13:54.072 --> 00:13:55.241
Uh

148
00:13:55.579 --> 00:13:59.563
Look, there is nothing magical
about hydrogen and oxygen.

149
00:13:59.588 --> 00:14:01.882
Water is required
for life on Earth, sure.

150
00:14:01.907 --> 00:14:03.563
But a completely
different planet

151
00:14:03.588 --> 00:14:04.925
might have completely
different conditions.

152
00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:06.780
I don't know why that
makes me such a nut.

153
00:14:06.972 --> 00:14:08.658
I need you to come with us.

154
00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.046
- Sorry, I didn't catch your name.
- Carl.

155
00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.105
- Carl. Hi.
- Dr. Grace,

156
00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.875
Petrova line samples
splashed down last night.

157
00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.781
I want you to tell me what
they are, how they work.

158
00:14:18.996 --> 00:14:21.720
I am a teacher at
Grover Cleveland Middle.

159
00:14:21.745 --> 00:14:23.602
And you have a doctorate
in molecular biology.

160
00:14:23.627 --> 00:14:27.821
And I ride a bike to work,
and it's not for exercise.

161
00:14:27.846 --> 00:14:29.713
So I'm sure there
are thousands of other

162
00:14:29.738 --> 00:14:30.664
- people who are better--
- It survives on

163
00:14:30.689 --> 00:14:31.795
the surface of the sun.

164
00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.452
Does that sound like a
water-based lifeform to you?

165
00:14:41.218 --> 00:14:42.753
The sun's really
dying, isn't it?

166
00:14:42.927 --> 00:14:44.003
Yeah.

167
00:14:48.187 --> 00:14:51.628
Is all of this necessary?

168
00:14:52.683 --> 00:14:54.085
Yes.

169
00:14:54.132 --> 00:14:56.431
<i>Please analyze
the Petrova sample.</i>

170
00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:57.654
Um

171
00:14:58.534 --> 00:15:00.940
<i>Just one thing.</i> The entire
room is filled with argon.

172
00:15:00.965 --> 00:15:02.800
Just try not to rip your suit.

173
00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:05.069
Uh

174
00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.800
Am I expendable? Is
that why you want me?

175
00:15:09.825 --> 00:15:11.538
That's not the only reason.

176
00:15:11.563 --> 00:15:14.468
It's almost like you
don't care if I die.

177
00:15:18.201 --> 00:15:20.277
Hold on. You have
to talk about it?

178
00:15:21.104 --> 00:15:25.950
The consensus here is that it
would be preferable if you did not die.

179
00:15:26.871 --> 00:15:28.123
Thanks, guys.

180
00:15:35.505 --> 00:15:36.708
Oh.

181
00:15:41.798 --> 00:15:43.803
Welcome to Earth.

182
00:15:58.736 --> 00:16:00.209
Are they alive?

183
00:16:00.865 --> 00:16:02.395
They're moving.

184
00:16:02.571 --> 00:16:03.940
So they're alive.

185
00:16:04.423 --> 00:16:07.440
Yes, they're moving, but that
could be for many reasons.

186
00:16:07.465 --> 00:16:08.998
What are they made of?

187
00:16:09.023 --> 00:16:10.573
It's gonna take a long
ti-- It took like...

188
00:16:10.598 --> 00:16:13.307
200 years to figure out how
bacteria works, so...

189
00:16:13.332 --> 00:16:15.166
Please do it faster.

190
00:16:17.382 --> 00:16:20.137
I'm just going through the
whole light spectrum here.

191
00:16:20.999 --> 00:16:22.628
Wow!

192
00:16:23.929 --> 00:16:25.780
You're not gonna believe this.

193
00:16:28.486 --> 00:16:29.786
Nothing happened.

194
00:16:35.572 --> 00:16:37.801
I can't see into these buggers.

195
00:16:39.149 --> 00:16:40.295
Amazing.

196
00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:44.498
X-rays, microwaves,
gamma rays, visible light.

197
00:16:44.523 --> 00:16:45.798
Nothing!

198
00:16:47.211 --> 00:16:50.414
Ooh, ooh, ooh, I've been
dying to do this all day!

199
00:17:07.164 --> 00:17:08.393
It's a cell!

200
00:17:08.418 --> 00:17:10.852
It's a cell. Wake
up! It's a cell!

201
00:17:11.288 --> 00:17:14.559
It's a tiny alien
cell. You guys!

202
00:17:14.658 --> 00:17:15.703
Applause.

203
00:17:15.728 --> 00:17:18.864
Guys! This is first contact!

204
00:17:19.804 --> 00:17:22.606
With life outside of the--!

205
00:17:22.813 --> 00:17:24.215
Uh-oh.

206
00:17:24.814 --> 00:17:26.542
Oh, it died.

207
00:17:26.942 --> 00:17:27.875
What?

208
00:17:28.713 --> 00:17:30.182
This is great news.

209
00:17:30.207 --> 00:17:32.508
Now we can find out
what they're made of.

210
00:17:35.178 --> 00:17:37.114
You guys fired up?

211
00:17:40.018 --> 00:17:41.452
Yes!

212
00:17:41.871 --> 00:17:43.074
Right?

213
00:17:43.935 --> 00:17:45.536
Carbon.

214
00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:47.711
Oxygen.

215
00:17:49.827 --> 00:17:51.596
Hydrogen.

216
00:17:52.061 --> 00:17:53.564
Oh.

217
00:18:07.538 --> 00:18:10.711
It's made....

218
00:18:10.736 --> 00:18:15.283
almost entirely...

219
00:18:15.308 --> 00:18:16.976
of water.

220
00:18:26.277 --> 00:18:27.688
All right.

221
00:18:30.435 --> 00:18:32.804
Sorry if I overreacted in there.

222
00:18:33.304 --> 00:18:35.870
Just, uh, realizing I was wrong

223
00:18:35.895 --> 00:18:37.916
about the only
original idea I ever had.

224
00:18:37.941 --> 00:18:39.619
Yeah. What else did you learn?

225
00:18:39.907 --> 00:18:42.081
They give off infrared
light when they move.

226
00:18:42.233 --> 00:18:43.908
- Mm-hm.
- Like, so much.

227
00:18:43.933 --> 00:18:45.803
I don't know how they
store all that energy.

228
00:18:45.828 --> 00:18:51.210
But, uh, their wavelength is
exactly the Petrova frequency.

229
00:18:51.235 --> 00:18:52.734
The light is how they move.

230
00:18:52.759 --> 00:18:56.317
Yeah, they consume the sun's energy,
and then they expel it for propulsion.

231
00:18:56.933 --> 00:18:58.737
They toot to scoot basically.

232
00:19:00.772 --> 00:19:02.340
Why did it go to Venus?

233
00:19:04.329 --> 00:19:05.896
I don't know.

234
00:19:07.137 --> 00:19:08.907
Okay. We'll tell you.

235
00:19:09.125 --> 00:19:09.983
What?

236
00:19:10.008 --> 00:19:14.348
I have 347 other biologists in 21
countries mobilizing as we speak.

237
00:19:14.373 --> 00:19:17.073
So thank you for your
help. I'm glad you didn't die.

238
00:19:17.098 --> 00:19:18.284
Let's pack this up.

239
00:19:19.336 --> 00:19:20.368
Pack up!

240
00:19:23.086 --> 00:19:24.325
That's it?

241
00:19:24.789 --> 00:19:26.591
- That's it, Carl?
- Talk to her.

242
00:19:26.758 --> 00:19:28.136
You're taking all the stuff?

243
00:19:28.161 --> 00:19:29.665
At the school you said
there were thousands

244
00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:31.740
of people more
qualified than you, so

245
00:19:31.765 --> 00:19:33.779
- I was being modest.
- I don't need modest.

246
00:19:33.804 --> 00:19:35.488
I need people who
think they're right

247
00:19:35.513 --> 00:19:37.043
when everyone else
thinks they're wrong.

248
00:19:37.068 --> 00:19:38.700
I need people who
piss other people off.

249
00:19:38.725 --> 00:19:39.507
That's me!

250
00:19:39.532 --> 00:19:41.193
Do you want to be on a project?

251
00:19:41.873 --> 00:19:43.657
If you...

252
00:19:43.682 --> 00:19:46.651
If you think I can help, then...

253
00:19:46.676 --> 00:19:48.318
Just answer the question.

254
00:19:49.052 --> 00:19:50.761
I wanna help the world.

255
00:19:50.786 --> 00:19:52.355
I left you three dots.

256
00:19:52.903 --> 00:19:53.936
Just three, huh?

257
00:19:53.961 --> 00:19:55.531
Plus the one you killed.

258
00:19:56.227 --> 00:19:57.929
I would take the three.

259
00:20:00.158 --> 00:20:01.853
- Yes?
- I'll take the dots.

260
00:20:01.878 --> 00:20:03.581
Get to work, Dr. Grace.

261
00:20:05.041 --> 00:20:07.063
The world is counting on you.

262
00:21:14.918 --> 00:21:16.487
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

263
00:21:17.535 --> 00:21:18.735
This is Carl.

264
00:21:18.908 --> 00:21:23.114
Imagine for a second that you're
an interstellar microorganism.

265
00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.131
I'm not doing that.

266
00:21:26.358 --> 00:21:29.228
Why would you leave
the sun and go to Venus?

267
00:21:29.747 --> 00:21:32.750
Why not stop at
Mercury? It's closer.

268
00:21:33.957 --> 00:21:35.257
Maybe fresh air?

269
00:21:35.282 --> 00:21:37.414
Venus's atmosphere
is mostly CO2.

270
00:21:37.439 --> 00:21:39.041
Maybe that's fresh air to them.

271
00:21:39.144 --> 00:21:41.458
You don't know what
these little space dots like.

272
00:21:46.571 --> 00:21:47.705
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

273
00:21:48.072 --> 00:21:49.806
Do we have an expense account?

274
00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.270
We don't.

275
00:21:56.026 --> 00:21:57.361
But I do.

276
00:22:27.966 --> 00:22:29.612
All right, I got
something for you.

277
00:22:40.452 --> 00:22:41.466
Thank you.

278
00:22:41.491 --> 00:22:43.570
- Do you have a coupon?
- We don't need a coupon.

279
00:22:43.595 --> 00:22:45.760
- We're the government.
- Which government?

280
00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:47.325
All of 'em.

281
00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:52.196
- So this box is Venus.
- The box is Venus.

282
00:22:52.221 --> 00:22:54.389
It's set to emit the same
infrared light frequency

283
00:22:54.414 --> 00:22:56.310
as Venus's CO2 atmosphere.

284
00:22:56.335 --> 00:22:58.865
So if the dots move
when you turn the lights on,

285
00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:01.071
that means they're
looking for CO2.

286
00:23:01.096 --> 00:23:03.488
Thus proving the
Carl Hypothesis.

287
00:23:03.513 --> 00:23:04.514
Sick.

288
00:23:06.911 --> 00:23:08.311
Sorry.

289
00:23:08.666 --> 00:23:09.958
- All right.
- All right.

290
00:23:09.983 --> 00:23:11.068
You ready?

291
00:23:15.152 --> 00:23:16.307
Uh-oh.

292
00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:18.149
Why you saying "Uh-oh"?

293
00:23:18.174 --> 00:23:20.505
The Astrophage
moved off the slide.

294
00:23:20.671 --> 00:23:21.625
Huh?

295
00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.309
We lost them.

296
00:23:23.334 --> 00:23:25.470
What do you mean, we
lost them? Go get them.

297
00:23:26.109 --> 00:23:27.711
If I open the door in the light,

298
00:23:27.736 --> 00:23:29.500
they're gonna escape, and
then we'll lose them forever.

299
00:23:29.525 --> 00:23:30.859
At least now they're in the box.

300
00:23:30.884 --> 00:23:32.309
So we'll just
turn the lights off.

301
00:23:32.334 --> 00:23:34.406
But there'll still be light.
It's gotta be total darkness.

302
00:23:34.431 --> 00:23:36.901
Okay, so just put this
box in another box.

303
00:23:45.189 --> 00:23:46.591
Nice.

304
00:23:58.681 --> 00:24:00.591
Go ahead and close it. Over.

305
00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.108
I can hear you.

306
00:24:08.378 --> 00:24:10.648
Entering phase two.

307
00:24:16.094 --> 00:24:17.629
I'm in.

308
00:24:23.107 --> 00:24:25.033
<i>Please report. Over.</i>

309
00:24:27.584 --> 00:24:29.254
<i>There's one.</i>

310
00:24:30.595 --> 00:24:31.696
<i>There's two!</i>

311
00:24:31.867 --> 00:24:33.777
Come on! Get the third one.

312
00:24:36.147 --> 00:24:37.278
<i>Got sample three.</i>

313
00:24:37.303 --> 00:24:40.689
<i>The Three Musketeers.
The Three Stooges.</i>

314
00:24:43.533 --> 00:24:44.702
<i>Uh-oh.</i>

315
00:24:44.943 --> 00:24:46.654
What? What?

316
00:24:47.785 --> 00:24:48.875
<i>Are you sitting down?</i>

317
00:24:49.387 --> 00:24:51.159
No, I'm standing
like a grown man.

318
00:24:51.656 --> 00:24:53.343
<i>'Cause there's a fourth.</i>

319
00:24:54.305 --> 00:24:55.607
What?

320
00:24:57.615 --> 00:24:59.854
<i>We're fathers, Carl.</i>

321
00:25:02.126 --> 00:25:04.275
Now I'm sitting down.

322
00:25:07.364 --> 00:25:08.438
<i>Stratt.</i>

323
00:25:08.463 --> 00:25:10.502
Carl and I made a baby.

324
00:25:11.443 --> 00:25:12.737
<i>What?</i>

325
00:25:12.762 --> 00:25:14.678
We figured out how
Astrophage breeds.

326
00:25:14.833 --> 00:25:16.368
<i>Don't move.</i>

327
00:25:18.957 --> 00:25:20.458
Wheels up.

328
00:25:22.687 --> 00:25:25.393
You know, Carl, when Stratt
said she was sending a jet,

329
00:25:25.418 --> 00:25:26.845
I thought you meant
like a

330
00:25:26.869 --> 00:25:28.674
- PJ or something.
- I thought of it. I know.

331
00:25:29.152 --> 00:25:30.591
Take this.

332
00:25:31.375 --> 00:25:32.924
It's been a long time
since I've taken a pill

333
00:25:32.949 --> 00:25:35.001
from a stranger without
knowing what it was.

334
00:25:36.348 --> 00:25:38.383
Stop! Stop!

335
00:25:49.261 --> 00:25:51.590
Dr. Grace, how was your flight?

336
00:25:56.854 --> 00:25:58.388
- Where are we?
- The ocean.

337
00:25:58.413 --> 00:26:00.996
The Astrophage reproduction.
Can you do it at scale?

338
00:26:01.021 --> 00:26:05.351
Oh, in theory, you just get a
bunch of elbow-shaped pipe,

339
00:26:05.376 --> 00:26:06.677
put sunlight in one end--

340
00:26:06.702 --> 00:26:08.037
Excuse me for a second.

341
00:26:08.987 --> 00:26:10.629
- Write it down.
- Yes, ma'am.

342
00:26:11.603 --> 00:26:15.580
and IR light and CO2
in the other. Repeat.

343
00:26:15.605 --> 00:26:17.426
- Anything else you need for--
- Just some water.

344
00:26:17.451 --> 00:26:19.161
He gave me a pill and
it's still in my throat.

345
00:26:19.186 --> 00:26:20.774
Two venti coffees.

346
00:26:20.799 --> 00:26:22.301
Thank you so
much. Water for him.

347
00:26:22.326 --> 00:26:23.426
Or coffee. That's

348
00:26:23.451 --> 00:26:25.933
- I need both.
- Or not.

349
00:26:25.958 --> 00:26:28.344
Okay, so this is
kind of a tough room,

350
00:26:28.369 --> 00:26:29.700
so don't take it personally.

351
00:26:29.725 --> 00:26:30.617
I don't need anything fancy.

352
00:26:30.642 --> 00:26:33.644
As long as there's a bed
and a shower I'm good.

353
00:26:38.835 --> 00:26:40.177
- I can't do this.
- Come on. Yes.

354
00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.045
- I can't. I can't.
- You're doing great.

355
00:26:42.533 --> 00:26:45.942
Everyone, this is Dr. Ryland
Grace from the United States.

356
00:26:45.967 --> 00:26:47.922
Please go here. Thank you.

357
00:26:47.947 --> 00:26:51.348
And he figured out how
to breed Astrophage.

358
00:26:56.849 --> 00:26:57.863
How did you do it?

359
00:26:57.888 --> 00:26:59.674
How long does the process take?

360
00:26:59.699 --> 00:27:02.235
Does it reproduce
by mitosis or meiosis?

361
00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.662
<i>What is the incubation period?</i>

362
00:27:05.717 --> 00:27:07.361
Um

363
00:27:08.787 --> 00:27:11.706
Carl and I made a mini
Venus out of a plywood box,

364
00:27:11.731 --> 00:27:14.493
and as soon as the
samples recognized

365
00:27:14.518 --> 00:27:19.725
the, uh, spectral signature
of CO2, they were....

366
00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:21.821
They were like,
"Whoomp! There it is!"

367
00:27:21.846 --> 00:27:22.574
Okay.

368
00:27:22.965 --> 00:27:24.924
- You can sit down.
- Who's Carl?

369
00:27:24.949 --> 00:27:27.346
While your experiment
was crude and clumsy,

370
00:27:27.371 --> 00:27:30.652
our scientists have
replicated his results.

371
00:27:32.436 --> 00:27:34.698
I'm sorry, how did
you see, how did you?

372
00:27:34.723 --> 00:27:38.133
We estimate an eight-day doubling
time under optimal conditions.

373
00:27:38.158 --> 00:27:40.353
- Dr. Grace is right.
- Oh.

374
00:27:40.378 --> 00:27:41.812
It is the CO2.

375
00:27:42.006 --> 00:27:43.341
That's why they're
going to Venus.

376
00:27:43.366 --> 00:27:46.186
- Please say it for everyone.
- That's why they're going to Venus.

377
00:27:46.257 --> 00:27:49.433
So that's why they're
going to Venus,

378
00:27:49.458 --> 00:27:51.293
is what Dr. Grace wants to say.

379
00:27:51.318 --> 00:27:52.887
They're going to breed.

380
00:27:53.036 --> 00:27:55.371
Whoomp! There it is.

381
00:27:56.394 --> 00:27:58.029
I really like him.

382
00:27:58.196 --> 00:28:00.766
How long to make
two million kilogram?

383
00:28:00.791 --> 00:28:02.458
Two million?

384
00:28:03.848 --> 00:28:07.914
Two million is-- Why....

385
00:28:07.938 --> 00:28:10.804
on earth would you need
that much Astrophage?

386
00:28:10.829 --> 00:28:13.164
Nobody told him?

387
00:28:13.464 --> 00:28:16.507
- He has no clearance.
- Please stand up, Dr. Grace.

388
00:28:17.082 --> 00:28:18.249
Stand up.

389
00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.188
I hereby grant you
top-secret clearance

390
00:28:21.213 --> 00:28:24.049
to all information pertaining
to Project Hail Mary.

391
00:28:25.188 --> 00:28:26.881
What's Project Hail Mary?

392
00:28:28.393 --> 00:28:29.694
All right.

393
00:28:33.097 --> 00:28:35.248
The sun is not
the only star dying.

394
00:28:35.293 --> 00:28:37.583
There is a clear
pattern of infection.

395
00:28:37.608 --> 00:28:40.775
Every star was infected
by its neighbor except one.

396
00:28:41.739 --> 00:28:42.952
- Tau Ceti?
- Tau Ceti.

397
00:28:42.978 --> 00:28:44.871
That's right. 11.9
light years away.

398
00:28:44.896 --> 00:28:48.606
It is uninfected despite being well
within the cluster of infected stars.

399
00:28:48.631 --> 00:28:50.683
- Why?
- Why? Tell him.

400
00:28:50.708 --> 00:28:52.560
We don't know!

401
00:28:52.585 --> 00:28:54.811
Which is why we
decided to build a ship

402
00:28:54.836 --> 00:28:56.572
to go there and find out.

403
00:28:56.768 --> 00:28:58.345
It's 11.9 light years away.

404
00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:00.496
You can't just build
an interstellar ship.

405
00:29:00.521 --> 00:29:01.527
Oh yeah, we can.

406
00:29:01.552 --> 00:29:03.595
The ship is not
really the problem.

407
00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.870
The energy required to
power the ship is the problem.

408
00:29:06.895 --> 00:29:08.263
It was the problem.

409
00:29:10.256 --> 00:29:11.983
The Astrophage is the fuel.

410
00:29:12.008 --> 00:29:14.292
Provided we can
make enough of it.

411
00:29:14.317 --> 00:29:17.287
And for that, we
need you, my friend.

412
00:29:19.184 --> 00:29:20.486
Me?

413
00:29:20.511 --> 00:29:23.811
Ooh. Those little guys
store a lot of energy.

414
00:29:23.836 --> 00:29:28.130
One mistake and that much
Astrophage could vaporize California.

415
00:29:28.155 --> 00:29:30.482
That's true, which is why
you now live on a boat

416
00:29:30.507 --> 00:29:32.950
in the middle of the
ocean in case "boom".

417
00:29:33.205 --> 00:29:35.273
- I live on a boat?
- You do.

418
00:29:35.506 --> 00:29:38.018
So you wanna build a
near-light speed spaceship,

419
00:29:38.043 --> 00:29:40.601
have it travel further than
any human-made object

420
00:29:40.626 --> 00:29:45.996
has ever traveled, and visit
a star just to "see what's up"?

421
00:29:46.021 --> 00:29:47.122
Yeah.

422
00:29:47.147 --> 00:29:47.915
And then what?

423
00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:49.996
There won't be enough
fuel for a round-trip

424
00:29:50.021 --> 00:29:53.491
so they send their findings
back to Earth on probes.

425
00:29:55.985 --> 00:30:01.631
And the astronauts...

426
00:30:01.656 --> 00:30:04.516
- die in space?
- Yes.

427
00:30:06.982 --> 00:30:08.020
Space.

428
00:30:11.671 --> 00:30:14.300
Any other plans
you've thought of?

429
00:30:14.325 --> 00:30:17.341
Anything else you're
kicking around?

430
00:30:17.366 --> 00:30:21.256
There are infinite possibilities
for this to go wrong.

431
00:30:21.281 --> 00:30:22.631
It almost certainly won't work.

432
00:30:22.656 --> 00:30:25.495
It's what you Americans
would call a long shot.

433
00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:27.420
Hail Mary.

434
00:30:28.126 --> 00:30:29.274
I get it now.

435
00:30:29.299 --> 00:30:33.333
The alternative is
to just do nothing...

436
00:30:33.358 --> 00:30:35.634
and to starve and
to kill each other

437
00:30:35.659 --> 00:30:39.764
and watch everything on this
planet go extinct, including us.

438
00:30:40.018 --> 00:30:41.484
So...

439
00:31:01.489 --> 00:31:04.064
That's a long time
to be in space.

440
00:31:04.089 --> 00:31:06.091
They'll be in
comas for most of it.

441
00:31:06.391 --> 00:31:07.958
But is that even safe?

442
00:31:08.093 --> 00:31:10.362
Nothing about this is safe.

443
00:31:24.876 --> 00:31:26.411
<i>We only need three.</i>

444
00:31:27.146 --> 00:31:28.247
<i>A pilot.</i>

445
00:31:28.506 --> 00:31:30.508
<i>An engineer.</i>

446
00:31:30.533 --> 00:31:32.367
<i>And a scientist.</i>

447
00:31:37.457 --> 00:31:39.192
Commander Yáo.

448
00:31:41.306 --> 00:31:44.208
I must have known you,
but I just don't remember.

449
00:31:44.836 --> 00:31:50.732
You make a funny face
in literally every picture.

450
00:31:52.143 --> 00:31:55.246
<i>You must have been very smart</i>...

451
00:31:55.271 --> 00:31:57.877
<i>and strong</i>

452
00:31:57.902 --> 00:31:59.470
and brave.

453
00:32:03.469 --> 00:32:04.769
Ilyukhina.

454
00:32:05.736 --> 00:32:09.043
First, I owe you
three bags of vodka.

455
00:32:09.874 --> 00:32:12.190
<i>You seemed to have
had a lot of friends.</i>

456
00:32:12.718 --> 00:32:19.417
<i>This picture of you,</i> what looks
like sneaking into the Kremlin is...

457
00:32:19.449 --> 00:32:21.328
legendary.

458
00:32:22.801 --> 00:32:24.936
<i>I wish you were still here.</i>

459
00:32:26.472 --> 00:32:27.913
<i>I wish I wasn't alone.</i>

460
00:32:28.399 --> 00:32:31.167
I wish I was...

461
00:32:31.192 --> 00:32:34.302
doing a better job, I just, uh

462
00:32:38.391 --> 00:32:40.559
You were both very loved.

463
00:32:41.868 --> 00:32:44.356
You deserve much more than this.

464
00:32:46.069 --> 00:32:51.564
I'll do my best to make
sure that you don't...

465
00:32:51.609 --> 00:32:54.618
you know, that you didn't--

466
00:33:05.998 --> 00:33:07.766
I'll do my best.

467
00:34:13.037 --> 00:34:15.506
<i>Approaching Tau Ceti orbit.</i>

468
00:34:15.531 --> 00:34:17.772
<i>Prepare for engine cutoff.</i>

469
00:34:17.797 --> 00:34:20.328
<i>- Fourteen, thirteen</i> ...
- What?

470
00:34:20.353 --> 00:34:21.468
- Why engine cutoff?
- twelve...

471
00:34:21.493 --> 00:34:22.758
- Seems like something
- eleven...

472
00:34:22.783 --> 00:34:24.297
- we should talk about.
- ten...

473
00:34:24.322 --> 00:34:26.097
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

474
00:34:26.122 --> 00:34:28.143
What? No. I'm not a pilot!
- six, five...

475
00:34:28.168 --> 00:34:29.851
<i>Four, three</i>...

476
00:34:29.876 --> 00:34:32.291
- What happens at zero? I mean--
- two...

477
00:34:39.618 --> 00:34:41.886
<i>You are now orbiting Tau Ceti.</i>

478
00:34:44.816 --> 00:34:48.587
What the fudging fudger?

479
00:34:51.336 --> 00:34:53.654
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

480
00:34:53.952 --> 00:34:56.254
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

481
00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:18.349
It's Tau Ceti.

482
00:35:28.713 --> 00:35:30.947
You have a Petrova line.

483
00:35:31.783 --> 00:35:33.531
But you're not dimming.

484
00:35:33.556 --> 00:35:35.191
Why?

485
00:35:45.377 --> 00:35:46.978
What is that?

486
00:36:04.383 --> 00:36:06.485
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

487
00:36:07.031 --> 00:36:08.725
What's a Blip-A?

488
00:36:09.241 --> 00:36:11.356
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

489
00:37:16.501 --> 00:37:19.564
No, no. No, no, no.
Let's go. Let's go, Mary.

490
00:37:19.589 --> 00:37:21.225
No-No-No-No!

491
00:37:21.347 --> 00:37:22.812
Pilot detected!

492
00:37:22.837 --> 00:37:23.949
<i>Please engage restraint.</i>

493
00:37:23.974 --> 00:37:25.214
Pilot detected!

494
00:37:25.239 --> 00:37:27.411
- <i>Pilot detected.</i>
- We're getting outta here.

495
00:37:27.436 --> 00:37:29.004
<i>Engage spin drive.</i>

496
00:37:29.029 --> 00:37:30.652
- Engage the spin drive.
<i>- Incorrect.</i>

497
00:37:31.336 --> 00:37:32.837
<i>Welcome to the</i> Hail Mary.

498
00:37:32.862 --> 00:37:34.332
Is it this thing?

499
00:37:39.044 --> 00:37:41.947
<i>Erratic maneuver detected.</i>

500
00:37:44.108 --> 00:37:45.377
Yeah.

501
00:38:01.306 --> 00:38:03.141
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

502
00:38:05.817 --> 00:38:07.819
What do they want?

503
00:38:37.916 --> 00:38:38.723
What?

504
00:38:52.386 --> 00:38:53.819
<i>Blip-B detected.</i>

505
00:38:53.844 --> 00:38:54.730
What's a Bl--?

506
00:38:54.755 --> 00:38:57.925
<i>Current distance from</i>
Hail Mary <i>is 800 meters.</i>

507
00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:00.097
It's sending me something.

508
00:39:02.461 --> 00:39:04.314
It could be a message.

509
00:39:07.039 --> 00:39:08.696
It could be a bomb.

510
00:39:09.363 --> 00:39:10.498
Is it a bomb?

511
00:39:12.005 --> 00:39:12.822
Shields up!

512
00:39:12.847 --> 00:39:14.632
<i>There are no shields
on board the</i> Hail Mary.

513
00:39:14.657 --> 00:39:15.579
Why not?

514
00:39:15.842 --> 00:39:17.476
<i>Thirty meters.</i>

515
00:39:18.187 --> 00:39:21.057
<i>Twenty meters. Ten.</i>

516
00:39:31.019 --> 00:39:34.483
Well, I guess there's egg
on both our faces, huh, Mary?

517
00:39:34.508 --> 00:39:36.064
<i>Blip-C detected.</i>

